#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632685 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & z \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 3 \\\\ z & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-z\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2-3z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632688 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(B)=2$, what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(B)=2$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =2 \\\\ 12-2x & =2 \\\\ -2x & =2-12 \\\\ -2x & =-10 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632702 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=45,$ what is the value of $t?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+t & 4t \\\\ -3 & 3-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+t)(3-t)-4t(-3) \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}+12t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+12t+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=45$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t+9 & =45 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+12t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-12t+36 & =0 \\\\ (t-6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632818 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{7/2}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7/2}}={x}^{-7/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-7/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/2+1}}{(-\\frac{7}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-5/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5{x}^{5/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632820 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}={x}^{-9/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-9/2+1}}{(-\\frac{9}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632821 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{9/2}}={x}^{-9/2}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-9/2+1}}{-\\frac{9}{2}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7{x}^{7/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632827 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/3}}={x}^{-8/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-8/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-8/3+1}}{(-\\frac{8}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/3}}{\\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{-5/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5{x}^{5/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632847 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/3}}={x}^{-5/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-5/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/3+1}}{(-\\frac{5}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/3}}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632865 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5p &=-10 \\\\ p+5q &=-17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-2$ into the second equation and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+5q & =-17 \\\\ (-2)+5q & =-17 \\\\ 5q & =-15 \\\\ q & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-2,\\,q=-3.$ So,\n\n$$\np - q=-2-(-3)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632873 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5b-3a &=8 \\\\ -4b+a &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4b+a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-5+4b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $a=-5+4b$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b-3a & =8 \\\\ 5b-3(-5+4b) & =8 \\\\ 5b+15-12b & =8 \\\\ -7b+15 & =8 \\\\ -7b & =-7 \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $b=1$ into $a=-5+4b,$ we can then find the value of $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =-5+4(1) \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=-1,\\,b=1.$ So,\n\n$$\na - b=-1-(1)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632876 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6y &=-16 \\\\ -3y+2x &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $y$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y & =-16 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=-\\frac{8}{3}$ into $-3y+2x=6,$ then we can find the value of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y+2x & =6 \\\\ -3\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right)+2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-\\frac{8}{3},\\,x=-1.$ This can be expressed as $(-1,-\\frac{8}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632885 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a+b &=-5 \\\\ -5a+3b &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b & =-5 \\\\ b & =-5-2a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5-2a$ into the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a+3b & =-4 \\\\ -5a+3(-5-2a) & =-4 \\\\ -5a-15-6a & =-4 \\\\ -11a-15 & =-4 \\\\ -11a & =11 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-1$ into $b=-5-2a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-5-2(-1) \\\\ b & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=-1,\\,b=-3.$ So,\n\n$$\na - b=-1-(-3)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186632889 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4y &=-2 \\\\ -2y+x &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $y$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =-2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $-2y+x=2,$ then we can find the value of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+x & =2 \\\\ -2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+x & =2 \\\\ x & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=1.$ This can be expressed as $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633605 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{g}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{h}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $\\mathbf{g}⊥\\mathbf{h},$ then $t=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =t\\cdot t+t\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-4t+4 \\\\  & =(t-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ (t-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633656 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2p + 3q &=-1 \\\\ 4p - 4q + 4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(p,q)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(p,q)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2p + 3q &=-1 && (1) \\\\ 4p - 4q + 4 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2)$, we can solve for $q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -4q + 4p &= -4 \\\\ -4q &= -4p - 4 \\\\ q &= p + 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $q=p + 1$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3q - 2p + 1 &= 0 \\\\ 3\\left(p + 1\\right) - 2p + 1 &= 0 \\\\ 3p + 3 - 2p + 1 &= 0 \\\\ p &= -4 \\\\ p &= -4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=-4$ back into equation $(2)$, then we can find the value of $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -4q + 4\\left(-4\\right) &= -4 \\\\ -4q - 16 &= -4 \\\\ -4q &= 12 \\\\ q &= -3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-4,\\,q=-3$, which can be expressed as $(-4,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633657 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-9u - 3v &=-18 \\\\ -4u - 4v + 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(u,v)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(u,v)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-9u - 3v &=-18 && (1) \\\\ -4u - 4v + 8 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1)$, we can solve for $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3v - 9u &= -18 \\\\ -3v &= 9u - 18 \\\\ v &= -3u + 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $v=-3u + 6$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -4v - 4u + 8 &= 0 \\\\ -4\\left(-3u + 6\\right) - 4u + 8 &= 0 \\\\ 12u - 24 - 4u + 8 &= 0 \\\\ 8u &= 16 \\\\ u &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $u=2$ back into equation $(1)$, then we can find the value of $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3v - 9\\left(2\\right) &= -18 \\\\ -3v - 18 &= -18 \\\\ -3v &= 0 \\\\ v &= 0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=2,\\,v=0$, which can be expressed as $(2,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633658 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - 9y &=-36 \\\\ -7x - 4y - 1 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - 9y &=-36 && (1) \\\\ -7x - 4y - 1 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3x - 9y &= -36 \\\\ -3x &= 9y - 36 \\\\ x &= -3y + 12 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-3y + 12$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -7x - 4y - 1 &= 0 \\\\ -7\\left(-3y + 12\\right) - 4y - 1 &= 0 \\\\ 21y - 84 - 4y - 1 &= 0 \\\\ 17y &= 85 \\\\ y &= 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=5$ back into equation $(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3x - 9\\left(5\\right) &= -36 \\\\ -3x - 45 &= -36 \\\\ -3x &= 9 \\\\ x &= -3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=5$, which can be expressed as $(-3,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633675 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4a + 4b &=24 \\\\ 4a - 2b - 6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(a,b)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(a,b)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4a + 4b &=24 && (1) \\\\ 4a - 2b - 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2)$, we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -2b + 4a &= 6 \\\\ -2b &= -4a + 6 \\\\ b &= 2a - 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=2a - 3$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4b + 4a - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 4\\left(2a - 3\\right) + 4a - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 8a - 12 + 4a - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 12a &= 36 \\\\ a &= 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=3$ back into equation $(2)$, then we can find the value of $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -2b + 4\\left(3\\right) &= 6 \\\\ -2b + 12 &= 6 \\\\ -2b &= -6 \\\\ b &= 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3,\\,b=3$, which can be expressed as $(3,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633676 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p + 6q &=-12 \\\\ 5p - 3q + 20 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(p,q)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(p,q)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p + 6q &=-12 && (1) \\\\ 5p - 3q + 20 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1)$, we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3p + 6q &= -12 \\\\ 3p &= -6q - 12 \\\\ p &= -2q - 4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-2q - 4$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5p - 3q + 20 &= 0 \\\\ 5\\left(-2q - 4\\right) - 3q + 20 &= 0 \\\\ -10q - 20 - 3q + 20 &= 0 \\\\ -13q &= 0 \\\\ q &= 0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $q=0$ back into equation $(1)$, then we can find the value of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3p + 6\\left(0\\right) &= -12 \\\\ 3p &= -12 \\\\ 3p &= -12 \\\\ p &= -4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-4,\\,q=0$, which can be expressed as $(-4,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634698 :question/problem "![](images/22/2261b100b72c98a99e76b33ee070dd8fe0dfecb030551d98e216b8ba85f7f0d0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $2\\sqrt{2},$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636640 :question/problem "If $a=⟨4,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-14⟩$, then $a-b=$ $\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a-b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na-b &= ⟨4,0⟩ - ⟨0,-14⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4 - 0, 0 - (-14)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4,14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636650 :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨-9,4⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨-15,5⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨-9,4⟩+⟨-15,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-24,9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636655 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨ - 12, 4⟩$ and $b = ⟨15,7⟩$. Find $a + 2b$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $a + 2b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b &= ⟨-12,4⟩ + 2\\cdot ⟨15,7⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-12,4⟩ + ⟨30,14⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-12 + 30, 4 + 14⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨18,18⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636660 :question/problem "If $a=⟨-13,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨-9,14⟩$, then $a-b=$ $\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a-b$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na-b &= ⟨-13,-1⟩ - ⟨-9,14⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-13 - (-9), -1 - 14⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨-4,-15⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636666 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{c}=⟨4,-4⟩$, then $\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ⟨4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637238 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1-2p & 4 & 2p+1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis $-126,$  find the value of $p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-2p & 4 & 2p+1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =(1-2p)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(2p+1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-2p)\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 2+(2p+1)\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =6-12p-8-40p-20 \\\\  & =-52p-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-126,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-52p-22 & =-126 \\\\ -52p & =-104 \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637312 :question/problem "Write the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(0,6)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=6$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-6)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y-6)^{2}=36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637331 :question/problem "Construct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }$\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{12}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }12\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637364 :question/problem "Find $a-2b$ if $a=-8i-8j$ and $b=-4i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a-2b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =(-8i-8j)-2(-4i-j) \\\\  & =-8i-8j+8i+2j \\\\  & =(-8i+8i)+(-8j+2j) \\\\  & =0i-6j \\\\  & =-6j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637374 :question/problem "Find $3a-3b$ if $a=-6i+4j$ and $b=-7i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3a-3b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-3b & =3(-6i+4j)-3(-7i-3j) \\\\  & =-18i+12j+21i+9j \\\\  & =(-18i+21i)+(12j+9j) \\\\  & =3i+21j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637378 :question/problem "If $a=3i+5j$ and $b=11i-11j,$ then $a+b=${{field-1}}$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a+b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na+b &= (3i+5j) + (11i-11j) \\\\\n&= 3i + 5j + 11i - 11j \\\\\n&= (3i + 11i) + (5j - 11j) \\\\\n&= 14i - 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637382 :question/problem "If $a=12i+j$ and $b=-11i-8j,$ then $a-b=${{field-1}}$i\\,\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a-b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na-b &= (12i+j) - (-11i-8j) \\\\\n&= 12i + j + 11i + 8j \\\\\n&= (12i + 11i) + (j + 8j) \\\\\n&= 23i + 9j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637390 :question/problem "Find $-3a + b$ if $a = 3i + 2j$ and $b = -9i - 11j$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $-3a+b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3a+b &= -3(3i+2j) + (-9i-11j) \\\\\n&= -9i - 6j - 9i - 11j \\\\\n&= (-9i - 9i) + (-6j - 11j) \\\\\n&= -18i - 17j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637626 :question/problem "Let the column vectors be\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$a=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$c=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFind $2a - (c - 3b)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - (c - 3b) &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}37 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637634 :question/problem "Let the column vectors be\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$c=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFind $b + (3a - 3c)$.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$b + (3a - 3c)=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb + (3a - 3c) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-13 \\\\ -28\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637645 :question/problem "Let the column vectors be\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$c=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFind $4b - (c - 2a)$.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$4b - (c - 2a)=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4b - (c - 2a) &= 4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637651 :question/problem "Let the column vectors be\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$a=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$b=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$c=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFind $4c + ((-4)b + a)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4c + ((-4)b + a) &= 4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+((-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}35 \\\\ -32\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637654 :question/problem "Let the column vectors be\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$b=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$c=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nFind $2c - (2a - 5b)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c - (2a - 5b) &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-40 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}38 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637666 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(M)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5-9\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =45-45 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637671 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =9\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0-8 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
